{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"insectile-modest-martin","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context\nRP-02 S3 T2 placed the interrupted-retirement repair call in `reconcile.py`'s load phase, at a position that is load-bearing rather than incidental: immediately after the under-lock staleness gate and before the pass's FIRST remote issue fetch. That leaves `reconcile.py` at **799 of the 800-line cap** in `.github/module-size-limit.txt`.\n\nTwo RP-03 tickets declare `reconcile.py` in their file impact and will rewire state advancement and exit status through it:\n\n- `diamond-flavoured-esok` (3350-90b1-2651-4c50) — RP-03 S5, \"Complete coordinator cutover and remove legacy write fallbacks\". Reason recorded: \"Make coordinator results authoritative for state advancement and exit status.\"\n- `postmodern-tangled-mollusk` (0f5d-2a24-91d2-4148) — RP-03 S5 T2, \"Seal versioned outcomes and proven-state advancement\", child of the above. Reason recorded: \"Advance state and exit status from proven coordinator outcomes.\"\n\nNeither can fit in one line, so whichever lands will have to perform a behaviour-preserving extraction on a file at the cap. That is the correct response per AGENTS.md (extract along an existing call-graph seam; never raise the limit) and this ticket does not ask RP-03 to do anything differently. It exists so the extraction preserves one specific ordering invariant that is easy to break silently.\n\n## The invariant that must survive any extraction of reconcile.py\nThe call `binding_store.repair_at_write_boundary(persist=persist, scoped=bool(scoped_ids))` must remain:\n- AFTER the under-lock staleness validation (`ensure_selection_current`), because a pass whose selection is stale has not established that its view is current and has no standing to repair; and\n- BEFORE the first remote issue fetch (`fetcher.fetch_snapshot` / `compute_snapshot`).\n\nThe pre-observation half is the one with teeth. A durable tombstone is authoritative retirement intent. A pass that fetched first could observe the retired issue answer 200 and then complete that issue's retirement in the same breath as fresh evidence that it is alive; repairing first keeps the recorded decision from racing a later observation. Move the call after the fetch and the repair still runs, still writes correct-looking bytes, and is silently wrong.\n\n## Why this is filed rather than left to review\nThe failure mode is invisible to almost every test. During RP-02 S3 T2 the mutation that relocates the call to a post-fetch position was seeded deliberately: it is caught by exactly two tests, both in `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_recovery_write_boundary.py`:\n- `test_repair_runs_before_the_first_remote_observation` — the behavioural oracle. It drives the real load phase with an event recorder and asserts the repair event precedes the fetch event.\n- `test_the_repair_is_invoked_from_reconcile_and_not_from_run_differs` — a structural anchor asserting the call is present in `reconcile.py` and absent from `run_differs.py`.\n\nBoth drive or name `_load_snapshots` directly. An extraction that renames or re-splits that phase will force someone to touch them, and the tempting shortcut under time pressure is to relax the assertion rather than re-anchor it. Relaxing it is the thing to avoid.\n\n`run_differs.py` is specifically NOT an alternative home: `reconcile._load_snapshots` performs the first remote fetch inside itself, and both `bind_operation_runtime` and `run_differs()` run after it returns, so no position inside `run_differs.py` can precede remote observation. That is recorded in ADR 0099 alongside the boundary itself.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] After RP-03 S5's change to `reconcile.py`, the repair call still runs after the under-lock staleness gate and before the pass's first remote issue fetch.\n- [ ] `test_repair_runs_before_the_first_remote_observation` still fails when the call is moved to a post-fetch position — demonstrated by seeding that move and observing RED, not merely by the suite being green.\n- [ ] If the load phase is renamed or re-split, the boundary tests are RE-ANCHORED to the new structure rather than relaxed, and the ordering assertion survives in a form that still discriminates.\n- [ ] `reconcile.py` remains within the 800-line cap without raising the limit.\n- [ ] ADR 0099's invocation-boundary section is updated if the realized position changes shape.","id":"d7b9-ac42-ad6f-4608","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["rp02","rp03","reconciler","coordination"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Preserve the pre-observation repair boundary when reconcile.py is extracted for RP-03 S5"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787155276249946001,"uuid":"1e8377f8-f303-457b-b239-74159d30239b"}